# Substantive commit messages

Use for commits where a coding agent did meaningful work or a **non-obvious** decision was made. **Trivial-only** edits (comment typos, format-only, no behavior change) may use a short 1–2 sentence message.

Full example: [`docs/notes/documentation_standard.md`](docs/notes/documentation_standard.md) §1. User-rule draft to paste into Cursor: [`docs/notes/cursor_user_rules/committing-changes-with-git.md`](../../docs/notes/cursor_user_rules/committing-changes-with-git.md).

## Subject line

`[scope] Short imperative description` — under 72 characters.

Scope examples: `[scraper]`, `[pipeline]`, `[model]`, `[feature]`, `[infra]`, `[eval]`.

## Body (required fields)

```
WHAT: Plain summary of what changed.
WHY: Problem solved or goal advanced.
HOW: Approach and why over alternatives.
ASSUMPTIONS: What must remain true. (mandatory)
OPERATIONAL INVARIANTS: Ordering, timing, idempotency, schema, rate limits, etc. (mandatory)
RISKS: What could break or fail silently.
TEST: How behavior was verified.
```

If **WHY**, **ASSUMPTIONS**, or **OPERATIONAL INVARIANTS** cannot be filled in, the change is not ready to merge.

Before drafting the body, run the agent debrief (`documentation_debrief.mdc`) while session context is still available.

Optional after required fields when co-authoring applied (`coauthoring_protocol.mdc`):

    CO-AUTHORING NOTE:
    ASSIGNMENT: ...
    HUMAN ATTEMPT: ...
    AGENT REVIEW: ...

(Legacy: `BRIEF` / `PLAN`, same as `ASSIGNMENT`.)

(Legacy labels `PLAN` / `HUMAN PASS` / `AGENT PASS` are equivalent.) See [`coauthoring-standards-v2.md`](docs/notes/coauthoring-standards-v2.md).
